 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to a license\nto sell liquor at retail for consumption on certain premises",
    "basePrintNo": "S7107",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7107-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §64, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7107\n\nSPONSOR: MATTERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to a\nlicense to sell liquor at retail for consumption on certain premises\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nRelates to a license to sell liquor at retail for consumption on certain\npremises\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Subdivision 7 of section 64 of the alcoholic beverage control\nlaw is amended by adding a new paragraph (e-15)\n\nSection 2: identifies the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe proposed local bill would amend the alcoholic beverage control law\nas it relates to the restaurant establishment \"Rockin Fish\" located at\n155 Main Street, Northport, NY, which has been lawfully operating at\nthat location for approximately 12 years without any citations or inci-\ndent. The owners of \"Rockin Fish\" own and operate another establishment,\nCrossroads Cafe for 29 years with a liquor license and have had no cita-\ntions or legal issues. The proposed local bill will, not apply anywhere\nelse in the State of New York. The bill aims to allow the sale of liquor\nfor on-premises consumption within approximately 185 feet of a religious\nbuilding. The religious building in question is the Trinity Episcopal\nChurch, located at 130 Main Street, Northport, NY. There are no expected\nfinancial implications, and the bill would take effect immediately if\npassed.  This bill will enable a singular establishment located in\nNorthport Village, Suffolk County, to be able to obtain a liquor\nlicense, despite being located less than 200' distance (approximately\n185') from a religious building.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
